    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 050446\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n946 PM PDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above-normal temperatures will continue across the region through \n  Friday, with dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* An approaching Pacific trough is expected to bring cooler \n  temperatures, increased winds and low chances of precipitation \n  next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through next Sunday.\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in good agreement that unseasonably strong \nridging will remain in place over the southwestern US for much of \nthe upcoming week. High temperatures will average 8 to 12 degrees \nabove-normal, while low temperatures will be 5 to 10 degrees above-\nnormal. While readings will be hot, HeatRisk for most of the region \nwill remain in the Minor-to-Moderate categories (levels 1 and 2 out \nof 4). However, areas of Major-to-Extreme HeatRisk (levels 3 and 4 \nout of 4) are expected in the lower elevations of Death Valley. \nHighs there are expected to reach around 110 degrees, with lows only \nfalling into the mid-80s, offering limited relief from the heat \novernight. As a result, an Extreme Heat Warning is in effect through \n8 PM PDT Friday for elevations below 2000 feet in Death Valley \nNational Park. \n\nBy next weekend, an approaching Pacific trough is expected to begin \naffecting our region. Given that this is nearly a week away, there \nis still quite a bit of uncertainty about what impacts we will see, \nbut the latest ensemble guidance suggests a noticeable cooldown, \naccompanied by gusty southwest winds and low chances of \nprecipitation. The potential for precipitation will depend in part \non the trough's interaction with Hurricane Rachel in the eastern \nPacific and how much moisture it draws into our region. The latest \nNBM shows a 15% chance of precipitation in the Sierra as early as \nSaturday afternoon, with chances ranging from 5 to 20% across much \nof the area Sunday. Some high-elevation snow (above 11000 feet) may \nalso fall in the Sierra in northern Inyo County on Sunday.  \n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds following typical directional patterns expected through Monday \nnight. Due to the light magnitude, the direction may vary at times, \nbut will favor the east-northeast during the afternoon. Can't rule \nout an isolated gust to 15 kts (20 percent chance) with the heating \nof the day. No operationally significant cloud cover through the TAF \nperiod.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light winds following \ntypical directional patterns expected through Monday night. Can't \nrule out isolated gusts between 15 and 20 kts with afternoon \nheating. With these gusts, the Las Vegas Valley TAF sites will favor \nthe east, KBIH the south-southeast, and KDAG the east. No \noperationally significant cloud cover through the TAF period.\n&&\n\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           MON, OCT 5  TUE, OCT 6  WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8 \n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record (Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     100(2024)   100(2024)   99(1978)*   98(2024)* \nBishop        94(1987)    94(2024)*   94(1965)*   95(1996) \nNeedles       112(1917)   111(2024)   107(2024)   107(2024) \nDaggett       104(2024)   105(2024)   102(2024)   101(1996)* \nKingman       99(2024)    100(2024)   99(2024)    98(1996) \nDesert Rock   100(2024)   100(2024)   99(2024)    98(1996)* \nDeath Valley  113(2024)   110(2024)*  109(2024)*  112(1996)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      MON, OCT 5  TUE, OCT 6  WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8 \n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record (Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     78(2024)    74(2024)*   75(2024)*   76(2024)* \nBishop        56(1993)    52(2018)    57(1943)    51(1960)* \nNeedles       79(1993)    77(2023)*   76(1947)*   76(2024)* \nDaggett       75(2024)    75(2024)    72(2024)*   69(1980)* \nKingman       67(1924)    67(1949)*   69(1947)*   67(1960)* \nDesert Rock   69(2024)*   66(1990)*   65(1980)*   70(2024)* \nDeath Valley  85(2024)*   83(1943)*   80(1943)*   85(1964)* \n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Planz\nAVIATION...Soulat\nCLIMATE...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
